// std/arena — generational arena for cyclic and graph data structures.
//
// An Arena<T> owns a pool of T values. Start here: create one with Arena.new,
// then alloc a value to get back a Handle<T> — a small copyable token (NOT a
// reference) that you pass to the arena to reach that value later:
//
//     var a = Arena<Node>.new()
//     let h = a.alloc(Node { ... })            // Handle<Node> — copy/store it freely
//     let n = a.get(h)                         // Option<Node>: None once freed
//     a.modify(h, (v: Node): Node => { ... })  // mutate the slot in place
//     a.free(h)                                // bumps generation → old h now stale
//     a.clear()                                // drops everything, frees storage,
//                                              // invalidates every outstanding handle
//     let f = a.freeze()!                      // build phase over: `a` is moved and
//     let n2 = f.get(h)                        // `get` is now infallible, no Option
//
// Reach for this over &T for graph/cyclic data: handles can live in struct fields
// and be returned, and each slot's generation counter turns a use-after-free into
// a None instead of silent aliasing.
//
// **Do NOT reach for it for an APPEND-ONLY pool.** If nothing is ever freed, no slot
// is ever reused, so the generation check can never fire — it is pure cost on every
// dispatch. What that shape wants is a distinct newtype per index family (`ExprId`,
// `StmtId`, …) so the type system stops you passing one where another belongs; the
// bounds check happens once, where the index is constructed. milojs does exactly this
// for its AST and measured +4.4% on a loop benchmark against generational handles
// (`docs/milojs-arena-safety.md`). Generational checking answers "is this slot still
// the one I meant?", and an append-only pool has already answered that.
//
// If the shape is append-only-then-read rather than append-only-forever, there is
// now a third option that keeps the arena API during the build and drops the check
// afterwards: `a.freeze()` consumes the arena and returns a FrozenArena<T> whose
// `get` returns T, not Option<T>. See FrozenArena below. It is refused for an arena
// that ever freed, which is exactly the condition that makes the check pointless.
//
// Worth knowing what handles do NOT answer: they catch a STALE reference, not a
// MISSING ROOT. If you build a collector on top of an arena, a value reachable only
// from a local — never handed to the arena — is invisible to a sweep no matter how
// its handles are typed. That is a different bug and needs a different mechanism.
//
// `get` and `modify` move whole values in and out of the slot, and that copy is a
// heap ALLOCATION for any T that owns heap — 20ms per 200k reads of a node with
// one Vec field, against ~0 for the borrowing forms. Unless T is plain scalars,
// use the borrowing pair, which hands a second-class ref to a closure and takes
// the answer out through a captured var. For a T that carries Drop or @noCopy the
// copy is not merely slow, it is a second owner of the resource, so those two are
// `@copyOut`: absent on such an arena (calling them says why), while every
// borrowing form below stays available. The same goes for FrozenArena.get and
// GrowOnlyArena.get, and the free-function spellings.
//
//     var edges = 0
//     a.read(h, (n: &Node) => { edges = n.edges.len })
//     a.modifyMut(h, (n: &mut Node) => { n.mark = true })
//
// That read/write pair is the ergonomic path. When the read produces a value,
// `let len = a.with(h, (n: &Node): i64 => n.edges.len)` returns it as an
// Option<i64> instead of routing it through a captured var. The read path is
// what decides whether a real program uses generational handles at all or
// falls back to a bare index into a Vec, so both spellings are here.
//
// T is one fixed type per arena. Heterogeneous data — an interpreter heap of
// strings, closures and tables — wants ONE Arena<Obj> over an `enum Obj`, not
// an arena per kind; otherwise every handle must also record which arena it
// came from. See the `impl Arena` block for the full API.

// A copyable token naming one slot in an Arena<T> — the arena's stand-in for a
// pointer. You do not use it directly: get one from arenaAlloc (a.alloc), then
// pass it to a.get / a.set / a.modify / a.free to read, overwrite, mutate, or
// release that slot. Safe to copy, store in fields, and return (unlike &T). T is
// phantom (not stored) so a handle can't be used with an arena of another type.
@derive(Eq)
pub struct Handle<T> {
    arenaId: i64,
    index: i32,
    generation: i32,
}

// A pool of T values addressed by generational Handle<T> rather than by
// reference. Allocation appends to `data` or reuses a slot from `freeList`;
// every operation is O(1) and none of them can dangle.
//
// The safety of a handle rests on two checks, one per field pair:
//
//   `id`    — a per-process identity stamped into every handle this arena
//             hands out, so a handle from a DIFFERENT arena (or from this one
//             before a clear) is rejected outright instead of indexing into
//             unrelated memory.
//   `gens`  — one counter per slot, parallel to `data`, bumped on every free.
//             A handle carries the generation current when it was made, so a
//             read through a freed-and-reused slot mismatches and yields None.
//             A slot that reaches i32 max is retired, never wrapped, so the
//             counter cannot alias an old handle by rolling over.
//
// Memory model worth knowing before you build on it: freeing recycles a slot
// but never shrinks `data`, so a long-lived arena holds its PEAK footprint for
// as long as it lives. `clear` is the only thing that gives storage back.
//
// Nothing here is a garbage collector. `free` is logical deletion — it marks a
// slot dead so the generation check can catch stale handles. The arena's actual
// memory is owned by `data` and released when the arena drops, like any other
// Milo value. If your object graph has cycles, reclaiming them is a sweep you
// write yourself over `handles()`.
pub struct Arena<T> {
    id: i64,
    data: Vec<T>,
    gens: Vec<i32>,
    freeList: Vec<i32>,
    live: i64,
}
// free() generation-checks the handle and returns before the decrement, so no valid
// double-free ever reaches `live = live - 1`. Stating it on the type is what lets
// arenaLen's `ensures result >= 0` be discharged instead of assumed.
invariant live >= 0

// Arena creation may happen on Promise.blocking workers, so identity allocation
// uses the compiler's seq-cst atomic primitive rather than a racy global increment.
var nextArenaId: i64 = 1

// Create a new empty arena.
pub fn arenaNew<T>(): Arena<T> {
    var id: i64 = 0
    unsafe {
        id = _atomicAddI64((nextArenaId.addrOf()) as *u8, 1)
    }
    var a: Arena<T> = Arena {
        id: id,
        data: Vec.new(),
        gens: Vec.new(),
        freeList: Vec.new(),
        live: 0,
    }
    return a
}

// Insert a value and return a handle to it.
pub fn arenaAlloc<T>(a: &mut Arena<T>, val: T): Handle<T>
ensures a.live == old(a.live) + 1
{
    if a.freeList.len > 0 {
        let fi = a.freeList[a.freeList.len - 1]
        a.freeList.pop()!
        let idx = fi as i64
        a.data[idx] = val
        // A negative generation denotes a reusable free slot. Restore its
        // positive generation before publishing the new handle.
        a.gens[idx] = -a.gens[idx]
        let gen = a.gens[idx]
        a.live += 1
        var h: Handle<T> = Handle {
            arenaId: a.id, index: fi, generation: gen
        }
        return h
    }
    let idx = a.data.len
    // Handle indices stay compact without allowing a narrowing conversion to
    // silently manufacture a negative or aliased slot number.
    assert(idx <= 2147483647, "arena slot capacity exceeded")
    a.data.push(val)
    a.gens.push(1)
    a.live += 1
    var h: Handle<T> = Handle {
        arenaId: a.id, index: idx as i32, generation: 1
    }
    return h
}

// Check whether a handle is still valid.
@mustUse
pub fn arenaValid<T>(a: &Arena<T>, h: Handle<T>): bool {
    if h.arenaId != a.id {
        return false
    }
    let idx = h.index as i64
    if idx < 0 {
        return false
    }
    if idx >= a.data.len {
        return false
    }
    return a.gens[idx] > 0 && a.gens[idx] == h.generation
}

// Free a slot, bumping its generation so stale handles are detected. A slot at
// maximum generation is retired rather than wrapped back onto an old handle.
@mustUse
pub fn arenaFree<T>(a: &mut Arena<T>, h: Handle<T>): bool
ensures a.live <= old(a.live)
{
    if h.arenaId != a.id {
        return false
    }
    let idx = h.index as i64
    if idx < 0 {
        return false
    }
    if idx >= a.data.len {
        return false
    }
    if a.gens[idx] <= 0 || a.gens[idx] != h.generation {
        return false
    }
    // Never wrap a generation back onto a stale handle. An exhausted slot is
    // represented by zero and retired permanently. Reusable free generations
    // are negative so every accessor rejects them without another side table.
    if a.gens[idx] < 2147483647 {
        a.gens[idx] = -(a.gens[idx] + 1)
        a.freeList.push(h.index)
    } else {
        a.gens[idx] = 0
    }
    a.live -= 1
    return true
}

// Get a copy of the value at a handle. Returns None if the handle is stale.
// Returns by value, not &T, because second-class refs cannot be stored in
// Option<_>. For large T, prefer arenaModify to avoid the copy churn. A T that
// carries Drop or @noCopy has no sound copy: this is rejected for it, use arenaRead.
@copyOut
pub fn arenaGet<T>(a: &Arena<T>, h: Handle<T>): Option<T> {
    if h.arenaId != a.id {
        return Option.None
    }
    let idx = h.index as i64
    if idx < 0 {
        return Option.None
    }
    if idx >= a.data.len {
        return Option.None
    }
    if a.gens[idx] <= 0 || a.gens[idx] != h.generation {
        return Option.None
    }
    return Option.Some(a.data[idx])
}

// Overwrite the value at a handle. Returns false if the handle is stale.
@mustUse
pub fn arenaSet<T>(a: &mut Arena<T>, h: Handle<T>, val: T): bool {
    if h.arenaId != a.id {
        return false
    }
    let idx = h.index as i64
    if idx < 0 {
        return false
    }
    if idx >= a.data.len {
        return false
    }
    if a.gens[idx] <= 0 || a.gens[idx] != h.generation {
        return false
    }
    a.data[idx] = val
    return true
}

// In-place update via closure. Avoids the manual get/modify/set dance and
// is the recommended way to mutate a single field of an arena value.
// Returns false if the handle is stale (closure not invoked). The closure takes
// the value by copy, so a T carrying Drop or @noCopy is rejected: use arenaModifyMut.
@mustUse
@copyOut
pub fn arenaModify<T>(a: &mut Arena<T>, h: Handle<T>, f: (T) => T): bool {
    if h.arenaId != a.id {
        return false
    }
    let idx = h.index as i64
    if idx < 0 {
        return false
    }
    if idx >= a.data.len {
        return false
    }
    if a.gens[idx] <= 0 || a.gens[idx] != h.generation {
        return false
    }
    a.data[idx] = f(a.data[idx])
    return true
}

// Read via borrow — no copy. The &T flows into `f` as a second-class ref:
// valid only inside the closure, never stored or returned. Returns None (and
// does not call f) if the handle is stale. This is the zero-copy alternative
// to arenaGet for large T — read just the field(s) you need inside f.
pub fn arenaWith<T, R>(a: &Arena<T>, h: Handle<T>, f: (&T) => R): Option<R> {
    if h.arenaId != a.id {
        return Option.None
    }
    let idx = h.index as i64
    if idx < 0 {
        return Option.None
    }
    if idx >= a.data.len {
        return Option.None
    }
    if a.gens[idx] <= 0 || a.gens[idx] != h.generation {
        return Option.None
    }
    return Option.Some(f(a.data[idx]))
}

// Read via borrow with a VOID callback — the same zero-copy read as arenaWith
// (`a.with(h, f)`), for a read that has nothing to return, or one whose result
// is easier to take out through a captured `var`:
//
//     var name = ""
//     a.read(h, (v: &Node) => { name = v.name.clone() })
//
// Why this matters more than it looks: `a.get(h)` COPIES the whole T out of the
// slot, which for any T with an owning field is a heap allocation per read —
// measured at 20ms per 200k reads of a node with one Vec field, against ~0 for
// the borrowing paths. That gap is why real programs reach for a bare index into
// a Vec instead of a Handle, so the ergonomic read path is the one that decides
// whether generational safety gets used at all.
@mustUse
pub fn arenaRead<T>(a: &Arena<T>, h: Handle<T>, f: (&T) => void): bool {
    if h.arenaId != a.id {
        return false
    }
    let idx = h.index as i64
    if idx < 0 {
        return false
    }
    if idx >= a.data.len {
        return false
    }
    if a.gens[idx] <= 0 || a.gens[idx] != h.generation {
        return false
    }
    f(a.data[idx])
    return true
}

// In-place mutate via &mut borrow — no copy in, no copy out, no full-struct
// rewrite. Mutate fields of the live value directly inside f. Returns false
// (f not called) if the handle is stale. Preferred over arenaModify when T is
// large or you only touch a field or two.
@mustUse
pub fn arenaModifyMut<T>(a: &mut Arena<T>, h: Handle<T>, f: (&mut T) => void): bool {
    if h.arenaId != a.id {
        return false
    }
    let idx = h.index as i64
    if idx < 0 {
        return false
    }
    if idx >= a.data.len {
        return false
    }
    if a.gens[idx] <= 0 || a.gens[idx] != h.generation {
        return false
    }
    f(&mut a.data[idx])
    return true
}

// Number of live entries.
pub fn arenaLen<T>(a: &Arena<T>): i64
ensures result >= 0
{
    return a.live
}

// Drop every value and release the backing storage in one go. Per-slot free()
// recycles a slot but never shrinks the arena, so a long-lived arena sits at its
// peak footprint forever; clear is the bulk-reset escape from that.
//
// Every outstanding handle goes stale because the arena takes a fresh identity —
// a pre-clear handle fails the arenaId check outright. Restarting generations at
// 1 on a recycled Vec would otherwise let an old handle alias a new value.
pub fn arenaClear<T>(a: &mut Arena<T>)
ensures a.live == 0
{
    var id: i64 = 0
    unsafe {
        id = _atomicAddI64((nextArenaId.addrOf()) as *u8, 1)
    }
    a.id = id
    a.data = Vec.new()
    a.gens = Vec.new()
    a.freeList = Vec.new()
    a.live = 0
}

// Snapshot every currently live handle. The returned handles remain ordinary
// generational capabilities: later frees invalidate them, while later allocs do
// not appear in this already-produced Vec. This is the safe enumeration shape
// for collectors because no element reference survives into caller code.
pub fn arenaHandles<T>(arena: &Arena<T>): Vec<Handle<T>> {
    var result: Vec<Handle<T>> = Vec.withCapacity(arena.live)
    for i in 0..arena.data.len {
        if arena.gens[i] > 0 {
            result.push(Handle {
                arenaId: arena.id,
                index: i as i32,
                generation: arena.gens[i],
            })
        }
    }
    return result
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// GrowOnlyArena and FrozenArena: arenas past their build phase.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// An arena that can still ALLOCATE but can no longer free, clear or reuse a slot.
//
// The middle tier, and the one most builders actually want. `freeze` gives an
// infallible `get` by ending the build phase; that is no use to a symbol table or an
// AST that is still growing. Removing only `free` is enough: a slot is never recycled,
// so no handle this arena ever minted can come to name a different value, and `get`
// returns T with no Option to unwrap — while `alloc` keeps working.
//
// Growth does not disturb it. Appending moves the backing storage but not the slot
// INDICES, and a handle names an index, so every handle minted before a growth still
// resolves to the same value after it.
//
// `freeze()` is the terminal state when the building really is done.
pub struct GrowOnlyArena<T> {
    id: i64,
    data: Vec<T>,
}

// Consume an arena into the grow-only tier. Refused, like `freeze`, for an arena that
// ever freed a slot: a freed-and-reused slot leaves stale handles naming a live slot
// holding a different value, and this tier removes the generation check that would
// catch them. The refusal hands the arena back.
pub fn arenaSealGrowth<T>(a: Arena<T>): Result<GrowOnlyArena<T>, FreezeRejected<T>> {
    var bad: i64 = -1
    var i: i64 = 0
    while i < a.gens.len {
        if a.gens[i] != 1 {
            bad = i
            i = a.gens.len
        } else {
            i += 1
        }
    }
    if bad >= 0 {
        var r: FreezeRejected<T> = FreezeRejected {
            arena: a, slot: bad
        }
        return Result.Err(r)
    }
    var g: GrowOnlyArena<T> = GrowOnlyArena {
        id: a.id, data: a.data
    }
    return Result.Ok(g)
}

impl GrowOnlyArena<T> {
    // Append a value and return a handle to it. The only mutating operation this
    // tier has: nothing here frees, clears or recycles.
    fn alloc(self: &mut Self, val: T): Handle<T> {
        let idx = self.data.len
        assert(idx <= 2147483647, "arena slot capacity exceeded")
        self.data.push(val)
        var h: Handle<T> = Handle {
            arenaId: self.id, index: idx as i32, generation: 1
        }
        return h
    }

    // The value at `h`, with no Option to unwrap. Infallible for any handle this
    // arena or the one it was sealed from minted: no slot is ever recycled, so a
    // handle cannot come to name something else. A copy, so absent for a T that
    // carries Drop or @noCopy (see the module comment); `read` is the borrowing form.
    @copyOut
    fn get(self: &Self, h: Handle<T>): T {
        assert(h.arenaId == self.id, "grow-only arena: handle was minted by a different arena")
        let idx = h.index as i64
        assert(idx >= 0, "grow-only arena: handle index is negative")
        assert(idx < self.data.len, "grow-only arena: handle index is past the end of this arena")
        let copied: Option<T> = Option.Some(self.data[idx])
        return copied!
    }

    // Overwrite the value at `h`.
    //
    // Mutating a slot's CONTENTS is not what makes a handle stale — only recycling a
    // slot is, and this tier cannot recycle. So a graph whose nodes keep changing is
    // exactly the shape that belongs here, and locking it out would have left the tier
    // usable only by data that never moves.
    fn set(self: &mut Self, h: Handle<T>, val: T): void {
        assert(h.arenaId == self.id, "grow-only arena: handle was minted by a different arena")
        let idx = h.index as i64
        assert(idx >= 0 && idx < self.data.len, "grow-only arena: handle index out of range")
        self.data[idx] = val
    }

    // Mutate the value at `h` in place, without copying it out and back.
    fn modifyMut(self: &mut Self, h: Handle<T>, f: (&mut T) => void): void {
        assert(h.arenaId == self.id, "grow-only arena: handle was minted by a different arena")
        let idx = h.index as i64
        assert(idx >= 0 && idx < self.data.len, "grow-only arena: handle index out of range")
        f(&mut self.data[idx])
    }

    // Borrow rather than copy. Prefer this when T owns heap storage.
    fn read(self: &Self, h: Handle<T>, f: (&T) => void): void {
        assert(h.arenaId == self.id, "grow-only arena: handle was minted by a different arena")
        let idx = h.index as i64
        assert(idx >= 0 && idx < self.data.len, "grow-only arena: handle index out of range")
        f(self.data[idx])
    }

    // Whether `h` names a slot here. Branch on this for a handle that might be foreign.
    @mustUse
    fn holds(self: &Self, h: Handle<T>): bool {
        if h.arenaId != self.id {
            return false
        }
        let idx = h.index as i64
        return idx >= 0 && idx < self.data.len
    }

    // Slots held; every one is live.
    fn len(self: &Self): i64 {
        return self.data.len
    }

    // Building is over: give up `alloc` too. Cannot fail, because this tier never
    // freed anything.
    fn freeze(self: Self): FrozenArena<T> {
        var f: FrozenArena<T> = FrozenArena {
            id: self.id, data: self.data
        }
        return f
    }
}

// A read-only Arena<T>: no alloc, no free, no clear, ever. You get one by
// consuming an arena with `freeze()`:
//
//     var a = Arena<Node>.new()
//     let root = a.alloc(Node { ... })      // build phase: alloc freely
//     let tree = a.freeze()!                // `a` is moved; build phase over
//     let n = tree.get(root)                // Node, not Option<Node>
//
// Staleness is not representable here, so `get` returns T directly instead of
// Option<T> and there is no failure path to plumb through every call site.
// That is not a promise the type makes on its honour: the conversion CONSUMES
// the arena, so the move checker rejects any later use of the source binding,
// and no path survives through which a slot could be freed after the freeze.
//
// The generation counters are not carried over because nothing can bump them.
pub struct FrozenArena<T> {
    id: i64,
    data: Vec<T>,
}

// Why a refused freeze does not eat the arena: `freeze` consumes its input, so
// a plain `Result<FrozenArena<T>, string>` would drop the caller's whole arena
// on the failure path with no way to get it back. The rejection carries the
// arena home instead.
pub struct FreezeRejected<T> {
    // The arena, untouched. Take it back and keep using it.
    arena: Arena<T>,
    // The first slot found to have been freed.
    slot: i64,
}

// Consume an arena and hand back a read-only view of the same storage. O(n) in
// slots to check the precondition; no element is copied.
//
// Refused when the source arena ever freed a slot, and the refusal returns the
// arena. That refusal is the whole reason `get` can skip the generation check:
// a slot that was freed and then reallocated leaves stale handles naming a LIVE
// slot that now holds a DIFFERENT value, and an unchecked `get` would hand that
// value back as though it were the right one. Silently returning the wrong
// value is worse than any rejection, so the freeze is refused instead and the
// caller keeps the generation-checked arena.
//
// A slot's generation is 1 from birth and only `free` ever moves it (to a
// negative value, or to 0 when the slot retires), so `gens[i] != 1` is exactly
// the test for "this slot was freed at some point".
pub fn arenaFreeze<T>(a: Arena<T>): Result<FrozenArena<T>, FreezeRejected<T>> {
    var bad: i64 = -1
    var i: i64 = 0
    while i < a.gens.len {
        if a.gens[i] != 1 {
            bad = i
            i = a.gens.len
        } else {
            i += 1
        }
    }
    if bad >= 0 {
        var r: FreezeRejected<T> = FreezeRejected {
            arena: a, slot: bad
        }
        return Result.Err(r)
    }
    var f: FrozenArena<T> = FrozenArena {
        id: a.id, data: a.data
    }
    return Result.Ok(f)
}

// Whether `h` names a slot in THIS frozen arena. False for a handle minted by a
// different arena. Use it when a foreign handle is a possibility you want to
// branch on rather than abort over.
@mustUse
pub fn frozenHolds<T>(a: &FrozenArena<T>, h: Handle<T>): bool {
    if h.arenaId != a.id {
        return false
    }
    let idx = h.index as i64
    return idx >= 0 && idx < a.data.len
}

// The value at `h`. Infallible for any handle this arena's source minted.
//
// The two checks that remain are not liveness checks, they are confusion
// checks: a handle from ANOTHER arena, or an index outside this arena's
// storage, has nothing to do with staleness and must not be allowed to read
// unrelated memory. Both abort with a named message rather than return a
// plausible-looking value.
//
// A copy, so rejected for a T that carries Drop or @noCopy; `frozenRead` borrows.
@copyOut
pub fn frozenGet<T>(a: &FrozenArena<T>, h: Handle<T>): T {
    assert(h.arenaId == a.id, "frozen arena: handle was minted by a different arena")
    let idx = h.index as i64
    assert(idx >= 0, "frozen arena: handle index is negative")
    assert(idx < a.data.len, "frozen arena: handle index is past the end of this arena")
    // Routed through Option for the same reason arenaGet is: returning the
    // element directly trips the `index-clone` lint AT THIS DEFINITION, where a
    // reader of a user program can do nothing about it. The copy is this
    // accessor's whole contract (see `read` for the borrowing form), so the
    // warning has no actionable target here. Cost is identical to arenaGet's.
    let copied: Option<T> = Option.Some(a.data[idx])
    return copied!
}

// Borrow the value at `h` instead of copying it. Prefer this to `get` when T
// owns heap storage, since `get` hands back a copy.
pub fn frozenRead<T>(a: &FrozenArena<T>, h: Handle<T>, f: (&T) => void): void {
    assert(h.arenaId == a.id, "frozen arena: handle was minted by a different arena")
    let idx = h.index as i64
    assert(idx >= 0, "frozen arena: handle index is negative")
    assert(idx < a.data.len, "frozen arena: handle index is past the end of this arena")
    f(a.data[idx])
}

// Slots held. Every one is live, so unlike Arena.len this is also the storage
// count.
pub fn frozenLen<T>(a: &FrozenArena<T>): i64 {
    return a.data.len
}

// Method API — same functionality, nicer syntax.
//
// These are the forms you will actually write, so each carries its own doc: the
// free function it delegates to is the same code, not a different behaviour.
impl Arena<T> {
    // A new empty arena with a fresh identity. Spell the type argument
    // (`Arena<Node>.new()`); a bare `Arena.new()` cannot infer T.
    fn new(): Arena<T> {
        return arenaNew<T>()
    }

    // Move `val` into a free slot and return a Handle<T> naming it. Reuses a
    // slot from the free list when one exists, otherwise grows the arena.
    fn alloc(self: &mut Self, val: T): Handle<T> {
        return arenaAlloc(&mut self, val)
    }

    // A COPY of the value at `h`, or None if `h` is stale.
    //
    // This copies the whole T out of the slot every call, and for any T that owns
    // heap that copy is an ALLOCATION, not a few words — 20ms per 200k reads of a
    // node with one Vec field, against ~0 for the borrowing paths. Reach for it
    // only when T is plain scalars or you genuinely want an owned copy.
    //
    // To read without copying, use `with`, which lends `&T` to a closure and
    // returns what the closure returns, or `read` when there is nothing to
    // return:
    //
    //     let edges = a.with(h, (v: &Node): i64 => v.edges.len) ?? 0
    //     a.read(h, (v: &Node) => { total = total + v.edges.len })
    //
    // Absent on an arena whose T carries Drop or @noCopy: the copy would be a
    // second owner of the resource. `read` and `modifyMut` remain.
    @copyOut
    fn get(self: &Self, h: Handle<T>): Option<T> {
        return arenaGet(self, h)
    }

    // Replace the value at `h`. False (and no write) if `h` is stale.
    @mustUse
    fn set(self: &mut Self, h: Handle<T>, val: T): bool {
        return arenaSet(&mut self, h, val)
    }

    // Update the slot by mapping its value through `f`. False (and `f` not
    // called) if `h` is stale. Moves T out and back, so prefer modifyMut when T
    // is large or you touch only a field or two. Absent when T carries Drop or
    // @noCopy, for the same reason as `get`. The closure's parameter is
    // immutable: rebind it as a `var`, change that, and return it.
    @mustUse
    @copyOut
    fn modify(self: &mut Self, h: Handle<T>, f: (T) => T): bool {
        return arenaModify(&mut self, h, f)
    }

    // Read the value at `h` by BORROW — no copy. Prefer this to `get` for any T
    // that owns heap (a string, a Vec, a nested struct): `get` clones the whole
    // value out of the slot on every call. False (and `f` not called) if `h` is
    // stale. Take the result out through a captured `var`.
    @mustUse
    fn read(self: &Self, h: Handle<T>, f: (&T) => void): bool {
        return arenaRead(self, h, f)
    }

    // Read the value at `h` by BORROW and return what `f` computes from it. None
    // (and `f` not called) if `h` is stale. The same zero-copy read as `read`,
    // for the common case where the read produces a value.
    //
    //     let edges = a.with(h, (v: &Node): i64 => v.edges.len) ?? 0
    fn with<R>(self: &Self, h: Handle<T>, f: (&T) => R): Option<R> {
        return arenaWith(self, h, f)
    }

    // Mutate the live value in place through `&mut T`. No copy in, no copy out.
    // False (and `f` not called) if `h` is stale. This is the write counterpart
    // to arenaWith and the right default for large T.
    @mustUse
    fn modifyMut(self: &mut Self, h: Handle<T>, f: (&mut T) => void): bool {
        return arenaModifyMut(&mut self, h, f)
    }

    // Logically delete the slot: drop the value, bump the slot's generation so
    // every outstanding handle to it goes stale, and offer the index for reuse.
    // False if `h` was already stale — so a double free is a no-op, not a fault.
    // Does NOT shrink the arena, and does not chase handles held INSIDE the
    // freed value; a cyclic graph still needs a sweep you write.
    @mustUse
    fn free(self: &mut Self, h: Handle<T>): bool {
        return arenaFree(&mut self, h)
    }

    // Drop every value and release the backing storage. The arena takes a fresh
    // identity, so every handle ever issued before this point is now stale.
    // The only operation that returns memory to the allocator.
    fn clear(self: &mut Self) {
        arenaClear(&mut self)
    }

    // Whether `h` still names a live value in THIS arena — identity and
    // generation both checked. A handle from another arena reads as invalid.
    @mustUse
    fn valid(self: &Self, h: Handle<T>): bool {
        return arenaValid(self, h)
    }

    // Live entries, not slots: freed slots awaiting reuse are excluded, so this
    // can be far below the arena's actual footprint.
    fn len(self: &Self): i64 {
        return arenaLen(self)
    }

    // A snapshot Vec of every currently live handle — the enumeration shape a
    // collector sweeps. Frees after this call invalidate entries in the Vec;
    // allocs after it do not appear in it.
    fn handles(self: &Self): Vec<Handle<T>> {
        return arenaHandles(self)
    }
    // End the build phase: consume this arena and hand back a FrozenArena<T>
    // whose `get` returns T rather than Option<T>. `self` is moved, so any
    // later use of this binding is a compile error.
    //
    // Errs if this arena ever freed a slot; see arenaFreeze for why that is
    // refused rather than papered over.
    fn freeze(self: Self): Result<FrozenArena<T>, FreezeRejected<T>> {
        return arenaFreeze(self)
    }

    // End freeing, but keep allocating. The middle tier: `get` becomes infallible
    // while `alloc` still works. See GrowOnlyArena.
    fn sealGrowth(self: Self): Result<GrowOnlyArena<T>, FreezeRejected<T>> {
        return arenaSealGrowth(self)
    }
}

impl FrozenArena<T> {
    // The value at `h`, with no Option to unwrap. Aborts only on a handle from
    // another arena or an out-of-range index, neither of which is staleness.
    // Absent when T carries Drop or @noCopy; `read` is the borrowing form.
    @copyOut
    fn get(self: &Self, h: Handle<T>): T {
        return frozenGet(self, h)
    }

    // Borrow the value at `h` rather than copying it. Prefer this when T owns
    // heap storage.
    fn read(self: &Self, h: Handle<T>, f: (&T) => void): void {
        frozenRead(self, h, f)
    }

    // Whether `h` names a slot here. Branch on this instead of `get` when a
    // foreign handle is a case you expect rather than a bug.
    @mustUse
    fn holds(self: &Self, h: Handle<T>): bool {
        return frozenHolds(self, h)
    }

    // Slots held; every one is live.
    fn len(self: &Self): i64 {
        return frozenLen(self)
    }
}
